      "knowledge_point": "Partial Derivatives as Limits of Difference Quotients",
      "problem": "Given that $f(x,y)={x}^{y},$ then $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=$",
      "worked_solution": "The derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is given by the limit \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{f(x,y+h)-f(x,y)}{h}).\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)={x}^{y},$  we have that \n\n$$\nf(x,y+h)={x}^{y+h}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{{x}^{y+h}-{x}^{y}}{h}).\n$$",
