    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-159611",
      "knowledge_point_id": "53c5d5d7-40d4-5210-8794-428cffc1f3f9",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding Partial Derivatives of Two-Variable Functions",
      "problem": "Find $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ given that $f(x,y)={x}^{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡y-{3}^{x+y}.$",
      "worked_solution": "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants.\n\nAdditionally, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡y-{3}^{x+y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡y)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({3}^{x}\\cdot {3}^{y}) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡y)-{3}^{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({3}^{y}) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡y-{3}^{x}\\cdot {3}^{y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\  & ={x}^{3}{sec}^{2}⁡y-{3}^{x+y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
