    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-35709",
      "knowledge_point_id": "53c5d5d7-40d4-5210-8794-428cffc1f3f9",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding Partial Derivatives of Two-Variable Functions",
      "problem": "Find $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ given that $f(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+xy.$",
      "worked_solution": "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants.\n\nAdditionally, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differentiation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+xy) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\cdot (2x)+y\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\end{aligned}\n$$",
